#! Cagara prelude — imported into every module automatically.
#!
#! The Rust core provides only the `__` primitives (relational operators,
#! window frames) and the `sql "..."` template mechanism. Everything below is
#! ordinary Cagara. Type signatures on `sql` templates are required: they give
#! the arity and whether the result is a scalar (`expr r a`), an aggregate
#! (`agg (expr r a)`), or a window function (`win (expr r a)`). All signatures
#! are checked by the type checker.
#!
#! A name defined more than once (each with a signature) is overloaded: the
#! type checker picks the definition whose signature fits each use.
#!
#! Functions take the value they operate on last, so a partial application is a
#! reusable transformation: `addDays 7`, `coalesce 0`, `contains "@"`,
#! `.name & trim >>> lower`.
#!
#! Each definition carries its own documentation below. `cagara doc --prelude`
#! prints this file as a reference, and `cagara doc <file>` does the same for
#! any other file that documents its definitions the same way.

# ── combinators ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The fixities are Haskell's, where these are the standard combinators: `$` is
# the loosest operator of all, application with its arguments reversed; `&` is
# `Data.Function.(&)`, reverse application; and `>>>` / `<<<` are the two
# directions of composition (`Control.Category`), which bind tighter than every
# other operator so `f >>> g $ x` is `(f >>> g) $ x`.
#
# The fixities themselves live in the core (`cagara-syntax`'s `ops`), not here:
# precedence is a parsing fact and has to be known before a file is read, so it
# is not something a module — this one included — can declare. What this file
# provides is each operator's *meaning*, as the `_&_` / `_$_` definitions below.

## Reverse application, `Data.Function.(&)`: `x & f` is `f x`.
##
## This is also the query pipeline. A stage is an operator at this same level
## (see the stage shorthands below), so `q & where p & select {.a}` is ordinary
## application written left to right: the query first, then what to do with it.
_&_ : a -> (a -> b) -> b = x => f => f x

## Application, `($)`: `f $ x` is `f x`, at the lowest precedence, so
## `coalesce 0 $ sum .x` needs no parentheses around the argument.
_$_ : (a -> b) -> a -> b = f => x => f x

## Forward composition: `f >>> g` is `g` after `f`.
##
## The natural spelling in a left-to-right pipeline — `.name & trim >>> lower` —
## and it binds tighter than every other operator, so `f >>> g $ x` parses as
## `(f >>> g) $ x`.
_>>>_ : (a -> b) -> (b -> c) -> a -> c = f => g => x => g (f x)

## Backward composition: `f <<< g` is `f` after `g`, the mirror of `>>>` — the
## same pair as `&` and `$`, for a composition written the other way round.
_<<<_ : (b -> c) -> (a -> b) -> a -> c = f => g => x => f (g x)

## The identity function: `id x` is `x`. Useful where a function is expected
## and there is nothing to do, such as a fold's initial value.
id : a -> a = x => x

# ── scalar SQL expressions ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Typed conditional: `ifThenElse p a b` is a searched `CASE WHEN p THEN a ELSE b`.
##
## Both branches are checked at the same expression phase, so it also works
## after `agg` or `select` when the surrounding stage permits that phase.
ifThenElse : expr r bool -> expr r a -> expr r a -> expr r a =
  sql "CASE WHEN $1 THEN $2 ELSE $3 END"

## Case-style alias of `ifThenElse`, for reading a chain as if/else. Multi-way
## conditionals are written with the `case { p => a; ... }` form instead.
caseWhen : expr r bool -> expr r a -> expr r a -> expr r a = ifThenElse

# Explicit casts are deliberately named by their result type. There is no
# implicit conversion between ordinary expressions.

# ── query pipeline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## A table: `table "schema" "name"` (the schema may be `""`). The row type is
## inferred from use — the projection stages decide the columns — so a
## definition of a table usually carries a signature naming them.
table : string -> string -> query r = primitive "__table"

## Filter rows: `where (.age >= 18)`. The predicate is non-null `bool`; SQL's
## three-valued logic never reaches it, because a nullable predicate must be
## handled first (`whereTrue`, `isTrue`, `coalesce`).
where : expr r bool -> query r -> query r = primitive "__where"

## Project a result row: `select { id = .id, label = upper .name }`, or
## `select {.a, .b}` for the plain projection. Columns not listed are dropped;
## `update` keeps them.
select : expr r (row s) -> query r -> query s = primitive "__select"

## `update {..}` is `select` merged over the input row: a name the input
## already has keeps its position and takes the new expression, and a name it
## does not have is appended. Columns not listed are passed through.
update : expr r (row s) -> query r -> query (merge r s) = primitive "__update"

## `omit "k"` drops one column: the row equation `input ~ { k : t | output }`,
## solved by the unifier's generic leftover rule rather than by a rule of its
## own.
##
## The two rows are spelled `r` and `s` — seemingly unrelated — because the
## relation between them *cannot* be written in a type. Which column is dropped
## is read from the application (`omit "a"` vs `omit "b"`), so both calls have
## the same type and no type-level former could tell them apart. Compare
## `prefix`, which can say `keyMap m r` precisely because its affix is carried
## in the type. `select` and `agg` are spelled the same way for the same
## reason: their result row is built from an expression, not from the input.
##
## The relation is enforced, not assumed — `t & omit "a" & select { .a }` is
## rejected with `no column `a`; available: b`.
##
## Unlike the projection stages, this signature is *not* compared against the
## primitive's internal type: `omit` is not a projection, so the annotation is
## descriptive. The constraint above is what actually types the stage, which is
## why writing something weaker here (or even wrong) changes no result.
omit : symbol k -> query r -> query (removeKey k r) = primitive "__omit"

## `mapValue "w"` transforms all field types using a wrapper:
## `"nullable"` wraps each in `maybe`, `"list"` wraps each in `list`.
##
## The wrapper is a label, like `omit`'s column name: `valueLabel w` is the
## parameter, the literal at the application binds `w`, and the result names the
## type-level term `mapValue w r` — the same binding on both sides, so the
## operation travels in the type and a stage's output row cannot come from an
## unrelated wrapper.
mapValue : valueLabel w -> query r -> query (mapValue w r) = primitive "__mapValue"

## `prefix "s"` prepends a literal string to every column name.
##
## The affix is a label, not an ordinary expression: `symbol k` is the same
## parameter `omit` takes, and the literal at the application is what binds `k`.
## That one binding reaches both sides of the signature — `removeKey k r` here,
## and the label-driven mapper `prependSym k` in the result. The checker decodes
## that mapper into the type-level witness `keyMap (prefix "s") r` and reduces it
## against the input row: `users & prefix "u_"` gives
##
##     query (keyMap (prefix "u_") { id = int, name = string })
##   ⟹ query { u_id = int, u_name = string }
##
## The witness in the result is the same label the argument named, so a stage's
## output row cannot come from an unrelated mapping. When the input row is still
## open — or the affix is a lambda parameter, as in `addPrefix = p => q => q &
## prefix p` — the term is *retained* rather than rejected, and reduces once the
## row and the label are known.
prefix : symbol k -> query r -> query (keyMap (prependSym k) r) = primitive "__prefix"

## `suffix "s"` appends a literal string to every column name. It shares the
## label-driven witness with its result in the same way as `prefix`.
suffix : symbol k -> query r -> query (keyMap (appendSym k) r) = primitive "__suffix"

## Group and aggregate: `agg { n = count, total = sum .amount }`. Inside an
## `agg` the argument is at depth 1 — a row expression, not a scalar — which is
## where the aggregate functions in this file apply.
agg : agg (expr r (row s)) -> query r -> query s = primitive "__agg"

## Sort: `order [asc .x, desc .y]`. NULLs sort last in either direction.
## Ties on the listed keys keep no particular order, since SQL does not promise
## one.
order : list (sortkey r) -> query r -> query r = primitive "__order"

## Keep the first `n` rows: `limit 10`. Applied to an unordered query the rows
## are whichever the engine returns first; order first if it matters.
limit : expr r int -> query r -> query r = primitive "__limit"

## Skip the first `n` rows: `q & offset 20 & limit 10`.
offset : expr r int -> query r -> query r = primitive "__offset"

## Drop duplicate rows: `SELECT DISTINCT`.
distinct : query r -> query r = primitive "__distinct"

## Membership: `inList [1, 2, 3] .id` is `id IN (1, 2, 3)`. The list comes
## first and the subject last, like every other predicate here.
inList : list a -> expr r a -> expr r bool = primitive "__in"

## `in` is `inList`, under the name a use site reads better with:
## `in [1, 2, 3] .status`.
in : list a -> expr r a -> expr r bool = inList

## Sort key, ascending — `asc .name` inside an `order` list.
asc : expr r a -> sortkey r = primitive "__asc"

## Sort key, descending — `desc .created_at` inside an `order` list.
desc : expr r a -> sortkey r = primitive "__desc"

## The grouping key: `agg { user_id = group .user_id, n = count }`. Every
## non-aggregate expression in the same `agg` is grouped by it.
group : expr r a -> agg (expr r a) = primitive "__group"

# Stage shorthands. Each is one definition, and that is the whole cost of a
# shorthand — nothing in Rust knows about `&+`:
#
#   _&^_ : query r -> query r -> query r = q => r => intersect q r
#
# The *spelling* is in the core's fixity table (`cagara-syntax`'s `ops`), which
# is what makes `&^` lex as one operator and parse at the pipeline's level. A
# spelling that is not there cannot be written, however it is defined here.
#
# The stage shorthands all sit at `&`'s own level, and that is what makes them
# pipeline stages rather than ordinary operators: the pipe level is not a
# special class of operator, it is `&`'s precedence, and anything at that level
# chains like `&` and binds looser than every expression operator.

## `q &? pred` is `where pred q`.
_&?_ : query r -> expr r bool -> query r = q => pred => where pred q

## `q &= fields` is `select fields q`.
_&=_ : query r -> expr r (row s) -> query s = q => fields => select fields q

## `q &+ fields` is `update fields q`.
_&+_ : query r -> expr r (row s) -> query (merge r s) = q => fields => update fields q

## `q &* fields` is `agg fields q`.
_&*_ : query r -> agg (expr r (row s)) -> query s = q => fields => agg fields q

## `q &. keys` is `order keys q`.
_&._ : query r -> list (sortkey r) -> query r = q => keys => order keys q

## `q &- n` is `limit n q`.
_&-_ : query r -> expr r int -> query r = q => n => limit n q

# Set-operation stages: `union`, `unionAll`, `intersect`, `except`. Each puts
# the query it is piped into on the *left*, so the stage and the direct call
# agree — `users &~ vips` and `except users vips` are the same query. See the
# note on `union` for why the bare `&` cannot do this on its own.

## `q &| r` is `union q r`.
_&|_ : query r -> query r -> query r = q => r => union q r

## `q &! r` is `unionAll q r`: keep duplicates.
_&!_ : query r -> query r -> query r = q => r => unionAll q r

## `q &^ r` is `intersect q r`.
_&^_ : query r -> query r -> query r = q => r => intersect q r

## `q &~ r` is `except q r`: the rows of `q` that are not in `r`.
_&~_ : query r -> query r -> query r = q => r => except q r

## Inner join: `users & innerJoin orders (.<id == .>user_id)`.
##
## The left side is the piped query. The predicate is a `bool` over the joined
## row, whose columns are `.x` for the left side and `.>x` for the right.
##
## The output has the left input's columns, then the right input's that the
## left does not have: on a shared name the left column wins. To keep both,
## rename one side in a `select` before the join (a field list is written by
## hand, so the new name is just the name you write).
innerJoin : query s -> expr (join r s) bool -> query r -> query (merge r s) =
  primitive "__innerJoin"

## Left outer join: every left row survives, and the right side's columns
## become `maybe` — they are NULL for the unmatched rows.
##
## The predicate is a join condition, not a `where`: a condition on the right
## side written after the join would drop the unmatched rows and turn the outer
## join back into an inner one.
leftJoin : query s -> expr (join r s) bool -> query r -> query (merge r (mapValue (AsNullable) s)) =
  primitive "__leftJoin"

## Right outer join: the mirror of `leftJoin`, with the left side's columns
## nullable instead.
rightJoin : query s -> expr (join r s) bool ->
  query r -> query (merge (mapValue (AsNullable) r) s) =
  primitive "__rightJoin"

## Full outer join: both sides survive, so both sides' columns become `maybe`.
fullJoin : query s -> expr (join r s) bool ->
  query r -> query (merge (mapValue (AsNullable) r) (mapValue (AsNullable) s)) =
  primitive "__fullJoin"

## Semi join: the left rows that have a match on the right, with the left row
## unchanged. `EXISTS` rather than a join, so a right row can never duplicate a
## left one.
semiJoin : query s -> expr (join r s) bool -> query r -> query r = primitive "__semiJoin"

## Anti join: the left rows that have *no* match on the right — `semiJoin`
## negated, and the idiomatic "rows without children".
antiJoin : query s -> expr (join r s) bool -> query r -> query r = primitive "__antiJoin"

## Union, deduplicated: `users &| vips`, or `union users vips`. Both queries
## must have the same row; the columns and their order come from the left input.
##
## It is callable directly or through the stage operator, and the two agree.
## The bare `&` cannot express it: `users & union vips` desugars to
## `_&_ users (union vips)`, and `union vips` is already a partial application,
## so the pipe would supply `users` as its *second* argument. `union`,
## `unionAll` and `intersect` do not care (they are commutative); `except` does,
## which is why the stage operators exist.
union : query r -> query r -> query r = primitive "__union"

## Union keeping duplicates: `users &! vips`, SQL `UNION ALL`.
unionAll : query r -> query r -> query r = primitive "__unionAll"

## Rows in both queries: `users &^ vips`, SQL `INTERSECT`.
intersect : query r -> query r -> query r = primitive "__intersect"

## Rows in the first query and not the second: `users &~ vips`, SQL `EXCEPT`.
## Not commutative, so prefer the stage operator or the direct call over
## `q & except r`.
except : query r -> query r -> query r = primitive "__except"

# Join operators, between `&` and every expression operator:
# `users & teachers ? .<user_name == .>teacher_name`. The `<` / `>` mark the
# side whose rows are all kept: `<?` left, `?>` right, `<?>` both. Their
# spellings are in the core table like every other operator; being at level 3
# rather than `&`'s level 1 is what makes them ordinary operators that build a
# query rather than stages.

## Join operator spelling of `innerJoin`:
## `users ? .<id == .>user_id` is `innerJoin` at operator precedence.
_?_ : query s -> expr (join r s) bool -> query r -> query (merge r s) = innerJoin

## Join operator spelling of `leftJoin`: the `<` marks the side kept whole.
_<?_ : query s -> expr (join r s) bool -> query r -> query (merge r (mapValue (AsNullable) s)) =
  leftJoin

## Join operator spelling of `rightJoin`: the `>` marks the side kept whole.
_?>_ : query s -> expr (join r s) bool -> query r -> query (merge (mapValue (AsNullable) r) s) =
  rightJoin

## Join operator spelling of `fullJoin`: both markers, both sides kept.
_<?>_ : query s
    -> expr (join r s) bool
    -> query r
    -> query (merge (mapValue (AsNullable) r) (mapValue (AsNullable) s)) = fullJoin

# `select {a = .a, b = .b}` is written `select {.a, .b}`. To recompute or
# rename a column use `update`, which keeps the row and merges over it.

# ── aggregates (depth 1: arguments must be row expressions) ───────────────
# Type variables in signatures are non-null; nullable inputs must be handled
# explicitly. The `*Maybe` forms are the explicit spelling for a nullable
# aggregate input: SQL's own aggregate functions already skip NULLs, so these
# pass the column through unchanged (`SUM(x)`, `COUNT(x)`) rather than
# coalescing it first. That matters for `avg`/`min`/`max`, where a sentinel
# would change the answer: over `[NULL, 5]`, `avgMaybe` is 5 while
# `avg (coalesce 0 .x)` is 2.5.
# `sum` / `avg` / `min` / `max` are NULL for an all-NULL or empty input, so
# they return `maybe`: write `coalesce 0 (sum .x)` for a number.

## `count` counts the rows of the group, NULLs included (`COUNT(*)`). A count
## is never NULL: an empty group counts 0.
count : agg (expr r int) = sql "COUNT(*)"

## `countOf .x` counts the rows where `x` is not NULL (`COUNT(x)`) — the
## difference from `count` is exactly the NULLs.
countOf : expr r a -> agg (expr r int) = sql "COUNT($1)"

## `countDistinct .x` counts the distinct non-NULL values of `x`.
countDistinct : expr r a -> agg (expr r int) = sql "COUNT(DISTINCT $1)"

## Sum of a column, overloading `int` and `float`. NULL for an all-NULL or
## empty group, so the result is `maybe`: `coalesce 0 (sum .amount)`.
sum : expr r int -> agg (expr r (maybe int)) = sql "SUM($1)"
sum : expr r float -> agg (expr r (maybe float)) = sql "SUM($1)"

## Arithmetic mean, overloading `int` and `float`; always a `float`, NULL for
## an empty group.
avg : expr r int -> agg (expr r (maybe float)) = sql "AVG($1)"
avg : expr r float -> agg (expr r (maybe float)) = sql "AVG($1)"

## Smallest value in the group; NULL for an empty group.
min : expr r a -> agg (expr r (maybe a)) = sql "MIN($1)"

## Largest value in the group; NULL for an empty group.
max : expr r a -> agg (expr r (maybe a)) = sql "MAX($1)"

## `sum` for a column that is already `maybe`: SQL skips the NULLs itself, so
## this is one `SUM` rather than a coalesce followed by a sum.
sumMaybe : expr r (maybe int) -> agg (expr r (maybe int)) = sql "SUM($1)"
sumMaybe : expr r (maybe float) -> agg (expr r (maybe float)) = sql "SUM($1)"

## `avg` for a column that is already `maybe`. It ignores NULLs — over
## `[NULL, 5]` this is 5, where `avg (coalesce 0 .x)` would be 2.5.
avgMaybe : expr r (maybe int) -> agg (expr r (maybe float)) = sql "AVG($1)"
avgMaybe : expr r (maybe float) -> agg (expr r (maybe float)) = sql "AVG($1)"

## `min` for a column that is already `maybe`; NULLs are ignored, and the
## result is NULL when nothing is left.
minMaybe : expr r (maybe a) -> agg (expr r (maybe a)) = sql "MIN($1)"

## `max` for a column that is already `maybe`; NULLs are ignored, and the
## result is NULL when nothing is left.
maxMaybe : expr r (maybe a) -> agg (expr r (maybe a)) = sql "MAX($1)"

## `countOf` for a nullable column: `COUNT(x)` already skips NULLs, so this is
## the same aggregate with a signature that accepts `maybe a`.
countOfMaybe : expr r (maybe a) -> agg (expr r int) = sql "COUNT($1)"

## `countDistinct` for a nullable column; NULLs are skipped by SQL.
countDistinctMaybe : expr r (maybe a) -> agg (expr r int) = sql "COUNT(DISTINCT $1)"

# Filtered aggregates apply a predicate to the aggregate's *input* rows, the
# portable spelling of SQL's `agg(x) FILTER (WHERE p)`. The predicate is a
# non-null row-phase `bool` and cannot contain an aggregate or window. The
# `CASE` omits `ELSE`, so a group with no matching row is NULL for the value
# aggregates and 0 for the counts, exactly as `FILTER` gives. When every
# aggregate in a stage shares one predicate, prefer `where` before `agg`: it
# filters the rows once instead of once per aggregate.
#
# The count forms use `COUNT(...)`, not `SUM(1)`: `SUM` is NULL when nothing
# matches, while `COUNT(*) FILTER` is 0.

## `sumFilter p .x` sums `x` over the group's rows that satisfy `p` — SQL's
## `SUM(x) FILTER (WHERE p)`. NULL when no row matches; use
## `coalesce 0 (sumFilter p .x)` for a number.
sumFilter : expr r bool -> expr r int -> agg (expr r (maybe int)) =
  sql "SUM(CASE WHEN $1 THEN $2 END)"
sumFilter : expr r bool -> expr r float -> agg (expr r (maybe float)) =
  sql "SUM(CASE WHEN $1 THEN $2 END)"

## `avgFilter p .x` averages `x` over the rows that satisfy `p`. NULL when no
## row matches.
avgFilter : expr r bool -> expr r int -> agg (expr r (maybe float)) =
  sql "AVG(CASE WHEN $1 THEN $2 END)"
avgFilter : expr r bool -> expr r float -> agg (expr r (maybe float)) =
  sql "AVG(CASE WHEN $1 THEN $2 END)"

## `minFilter p .x`: the smallest `x` among the rows satisfying `p`.
minFilter : expr r bool -> expr r a -> agg (expr r (maybe a)) = sql "MIN(CASE WHEN $1 THEN $2 END)"

## `maxFilter p .x`: the largest `x` among the rows satisfying `p`.
maxFilter : expr r bool -> expr r a -> agg (expr r (maybe a)) = sql "MAX(CASE WHEN $1 THEN $2 END)"

## `countFilter p` counts the rows satisfying `p`, NULLs and all — 0 when none
## match, rather than NULL.
countFilter : expr r bool -> agg (expr r int) = sql "COUNT(CASE WHEN $1 THEN 1 END)"

## `countOfFilter p .x` counts the rows satisfying `p` where `x` is not NULL.
countOfFilter : expr r bool -> expr r a -> agg (expr r int) = sql "COUNT(CASE WHEN $1 THEN $2 END)"

## `countDistinctFilter p .x` counts the distinct non-NULL `x` among the rows
## satisfying `p`.
countDistinctFilter : expr r bool -> expr r a -> agg (expr r int) =
  sql "COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN $1 THEN $2 END)"

# ── window functions (depth 1) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# spec = { partition = [..], order = [asc .x, desc .y], frame = .. }

## Row number within the partition, 1-based, in the `order` the spec gives.
## Distinct rows get distinct numbers even when the sort keys tie.
rowNumber : winspec r -> win (expr r int) = sql "ROW_NUMBER()"

## Rank within the partition: tied rows share a rank, and the next rank skips
## the tie (1, 2, 2, 4).
rank : winspec r -> win (expr r int) = sql "RANK()"

## Dense rank: tied rows share a rank and the next rank does not skip
## (1, 2, 2, 3).
denseRank : winspec r -> win (expr r int) = sql "DENSE_RANK()"

## The value of `x` on the row `n` rows before this one in the partition —
## `lag spec 1 .x` for the previous row. NULL at the partition edge.
lag : winspec r -> expr r a -> win (expr r (maybe a)) = sql "LAG($1)"

## The value of `x` on the row `n` rows after this one. NULL at the partition
## edge.
lead : winspec r -> expr r a -> win (expr r (maybe a)) = sql "LEAD($1)"

## Running (or framed) sum of `x` over the window. `wholePartition` sums the
## partition; `runningFrame` accumulates up to the current row. NULL when the
## frame is empty.
sumOver : winspec r -> expr r int -> win (expr r (maybe int)) = sql "SUM($1)"
sumOver : winspec r -> expr r float -> win (expr r (maybe float)) = sql "SUM($1)"

## Running (or framed) average of `x` over the window; NULL when the frame is
## empty.
avgOver : winspec r -> expr r int -> win (expr r (maybe float)) = sql "AVG($1)"
avgOver : winspec r -> expr r float -> win (expr r (maybe float)) = sql "AVG($1)"

## The number of rows in the window frame, NULLs included.
countOver : winspec r -> win (expr r int) = sql "COUNT(*)"

## The first value of `x` in the window's frame. With the default frame this is
## the partition's first row; with a moving frame it is the frame's first row —
## which is why a `lastValue` of a running frame is not the partition's last
## value unless the frame is `wholePartition`.
firstValue : winspec r -> expr r a -> win (expr r (maybe a)) = sql "FIRST_VALUE($1)"

## The last value of `x` in the window's frame; NULL when the frame is empty.
## See `firstValue` about the frame's effect.
lastValue : winspec r -> expr r a -> win (expr r (maybe a)) = sql "LAST_VALUE($1)"

## `ntile spec 4` numbers the partition's rows 1..4, as evenly as the row count
## allows — a quartile bucket per row.
ntile : winspec r -> expr r int -> win (expr r int) = sql "NTILE($1)"

## The relative rank of the row as a fraction strictly below 1: 0 for the first
## row.
percentRank : winspec r -> win (expr r float) = sql "PERCENT_RANK()"

## The cumulative distribution: the proportion of the partition's rows at or
## before this one, in (0, 1].
cumeDist : winspec r -> win (expr r float) = sql "CUME_DIST()"

# ── window frames ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## `rows start end`: a frame counted in rows rather than in peer groups —
## `rows unboundedPreceding currentRow` is the running total. The spec's
## `frame` field is where the two bounds go.
rows : bound -> bound -> frame = primitive "__rows"

## Frame bound: the start of the partition, with no limit.
unboundedPreceding : bound = primitive "__unboundedPreceding"

## Frame bound: the end of the partition, with no limit.
unboundedFollowing : bound = primitive "__unboundedFollowing"

## Frame bound: the current row.
currentRow : bound = primitive "__currentRow"

## Frame bound `n` rows before the current row: `rows (preceding 7) currentRow`
## is a seven-row trailing window.
preceding : expr r int -> bound = primitive "__preceding"

## Frame bound `n` rows after the current row, for a centred window.
following : expr r int -> bound = primitive "__following"

## The whole partition as a frame. Needed when a window aggregate should cover
## the partition rather than accumulate over it.
wholePartition : frame = rows unboundedPreceding unboundedFollowing

## The frame from the partition's start to the current row: the running total,
## spelled out because writing it is how a running aggregate is meant.
runningFrame : frame = rows unboundedPreceding currentRow

# ── scalar operators and functions ────────────────────────────────────────

## `a == b`, SQL `=`. NULL if either side is NULL: a comparison with NULL is
## never true, which is what `isNull` and `eqMaybe` are for.
_==_ : expr r a -> expr r a -> expr r bool = sql "$1 = $2"

## `a != b`, SQL `<>`.
_!=_ : expr r a -> expr r a -> expr r bool = sql "$1 <> $2"

## `a < b`. NULL if either side is NULL.
_<_ : expr r a -> expr r a -> expr r bool = sql "$1 < $2"

## `a <= b`. NULL if either side is NULL.
_<=_ : expr r a -> expr r a -> expr r bool = sql "$1 <= $2"

## `a > b`. NULL if either side is NULL.
_>_ : expr r a -> expr r a -> expr r bool = sql "$1 > $2"

## `a >= b`. NULL if either side is NULL.
_>=_ : expr r a -> expr r a -> expr r bool = sql "$1 >= $2"

## Boolean AND. Both sides are non-null `bool`, so SQL's three-valued logic
## does not apply here; a nullable predicate is made non-null first
## (`isTrue`, `coalesce`).
_&&_ : expr r bool -> expr r bool -> expr r bool = sql "$1 AND $2"

## Boolean OR. Non-null `bool` on both sides, like `&&`.
_||_ : expr r bool -> expr r bool -> expr r bool = sql "$1 OR $2"

# Arithmetic is overloaded on int and float (no implicit conversions). On
# ints, `/` truncates toward zero and `%` keeps the dividend's sign, in every
# dialect.

## Addition, overloaded on `int` and `float`.
_+_ : expr r int -> expr r int -> expr r int = sql "$1 + $2"
_+_ : expr r float -> expr r float -> expr r float = sql "$1 + $2"

## Subtraction, overloaded on `int` and `float`.
_-_ : expr r int -> expr r int -> expr r int = sql "$1 - $2"
_-_ : expr r float -> expr r float -> expr r float = sql "$1 - $2"

## Multiplication, overloaded on `int` and `float`.
_*_ : expr r int -> expr r int -> expr r int = sql "$1 * $2"
_*_ : expr r float -> expr r float -> expr r float = sql "$1 * $2"

## Division, overloaded on `int` and `float`.
##
## Integer division truncates toward zero in every dialect — `7 / 2` is 3 and
## `-7 / 2` is -3. The `CAGARA_IDIV` rewrite is what makes that uniform: SQL
## engines disagree about integer division, and SQLite returns a float.
_/_ : expr r int -> expr r int -> expr r int = sql "CAGARA_IDIV($1, $2)"
_/_ : expr r float -> expr r float -> expr r float = sql "$1 / $2"

## Remainder, `int` only. The result keeps the dividend's sign — `-7 % 2` is
## -1 — in every dialect, which `CAGARA_MOD` guarantees.
_%_ : expr r int -> expr r int -> expr r int = sql "CAGARA_MOD($1, $2)"

## String concatenation, right-associative and looser than arithmetic, so
## `"a" <> .b <> "c"` groups as `"a" <> (.b <> "c")` and `x + 1 <> "!"` needs
## no parentheses. NULL if either side is NULL.
_<>_ : expr r string -> expr r string -> expr r string = sql "$1 || $2"

## `concat a b` is `a <> b`, for a call site where an operator reads worse.
concat : expr r string -> expr r string -> expr r string = _<>_

## Boolean negation.
not : expr r bool -> expr r bool = sql "NOT $1"

## Arithmetic negation, overloaded on `int` and `float`.
negate : expr r int -> expr r int = sql "-$1"
negate : expr r float -> expr r float = sql "-$1"

## Uppercase a string, in the database's collation.
upper : expr r string -> expr r string = sql "UPPER($1)"

## Lowercase a string, in the database's collation.
lower : expr r string -> expr r string = sql "LOWER($1)"

## Strip leading and trailing whitespace.
trim : expr r string -> expr r string = sql "TRIM($1)"

## Strip leading whitespace.
ltrim : expr r string -> expr r string = sql "LTRIM($1)"

## Strip trailing whitespace.
rtrim : expr r string -> expr r string = sql "RTRIM($1)"

## `lpad 12 "0" s`: `s` padded on the left to width 12 with copies of `"0"`.
##
## The width comes first, then what to pad with, then the string — the same
## argument order as `left` / `right` / `substring`, which take their position
## or length before the string, so `lpad 12 "0"` is a reusable "pad to twelve".
## It is the reverse of SQL's own `LPAD(s, 12, '0')`, which is what the
## template below rearranges.
##
## A value already at least `n` wide is returned whole, not truncated.
##
## Unlike `length`, this needs no `CAGARA_*` rewrite: sqlglot passes `LPAD`
## through unchanged for every dialect. SQLite has no `LPAD` and will reject it
## at run time, which is the same gap `try*` has there — emitting the call is
## more honest than guessing at a `substr`/`printf` spelling, and the engine
## says so loudly.
lpad : expr r int -> expr r string -> expr r string -> expr r string = sql "LPAD($2, $1, $3)"

## `rpad 12 "0" s`: `s` padded on the right, the mirror of `lpad`.
rpad : expr r int -> expr r string -> expr r string -> expr r string = sql "RPAD($2, $1, $3)"

## The number of characters in a string — characters, not bytes, in every
## dialect (`CAGARA_LENGTH`).
length : expr r string -> expr r int = sql "CAGARA_LENGTH($1)"

## `like "%foo%" .name`: SQL `LIKE`, so `%` and `_` are wildcards. The pattern
## comes first to keep the subject last; for a plain substring test use
## `contains`.
like : expr r string -> expr r string -> expr r bool = sql "$2 LIKE $1"

## Case-insensitive `like`. Not every engine has `ILIKE`; sqlglot rewrites it
## where it can.
ilike : expr r string -> expr r string -> expr r bool = sql "$2 ILIKE $1"

## `substring start len s`, 1-based as in SQL and `len` characters long.
substring : expr r int -> expr r int -> expr r string -> expr r string = sql "SUBSTRING($3, $1, $2)"

## `left n s`: the first `n` characters.
left : expr r int -> expr r string -> expr r string = sql "CAGARA_LEFT($2, $1)"

## `right n s`: the last `n` characters.
right : expr r int -> expr r string -> expr r string = sql "CAGARA_RIGHT($2, $1)"

## `replaceAll from to s`: every occurrence of `from` in `s` replaced by `to`.
## (SQL's `REPLACE`, not column renaming — use `update` for that.)
replaceAll : expr r string -> expr r string -> expr r string -> expr r string =
  sql "REPLACE($3, $1, $2)"

## `strpos sub s`: the 1-based position of the first `sub` in `s`, or 0 when it
## is absent.
strpos : expr r string -> expr r string -> expr r int = sql "CAGARA_STRPOS($2, $1)"

## `contains "@" .email`: whether `s` contains `sub`. A plain substring test —
## `%` and `_` are not wildcards here, which is the difference from `like`.
contains : expr r string -> expr r string -> expr r bool = sql "CAGARA_STRPOS($2, $1) > 0"

## `startsWith "@" .domain`: whether `s` begins with `prefix`.
startsWith : expr r string -> expr r string -> expr r bool =
  sql "CAGARA_LEFT($2, CAGARA_LENGTH($1)) = $1"

## `endsWith ".com" .domain`: whether `s` ends with `suffix`.
endsWith : expr r string -> expr r string -> expr r bool =
  sql "CAGARA_RIGHT($2, CAGARA_LENGTH($1)) = $1"

# Math is scalar and non-null.

## Absolute value, overloaded on `int` and `float`.
abs : expr r int -> expr r int = sql "ABS($1)"
abs : expr r float -> expr r float = sql "ABS($1)"

## Round to the nearest integer, keeping the `float` type. Engines round ties
## differently; this leaves that to the target rather than pretending one rule
## is universal.
round : expr r float -> expr r float = sql "ROUND($1)"

## `roundTo 2 .amount`: round to `digits` decimal places. Digits first, subject
## last, so `roundTo 2` is a reusable transformation.
roundTo : expr r int -> expr r float -> expr r float = sql "ROUND($2, $1)"

## Round down to the nearest integer, as a `float`.
floor : expr r float -> expr r float = sql "FLOOR($1)"

## Round up to the nearest integer, as a `float`.
ceil : expr r float -> expr r float = sql "CEILING($1)"

## Square root. A negative input is the engine's error or NULL, not a
## documented value of this function.
sqrt : expr r float -> expr r float = sql "SQRT($1)"

## `power exponent base`: `base` raised to `exponent`. Subject last, so
## `power 2` squares its argument.
power : expr r float -> expr r float -> expr r float = sql "POWER($2, $1)"

## Sign of a number: -1, 0 or 1. Overloaded on `int` and `float`.
sign : expr r int -> expr r int = sql "SIGN($1)"
sign : expr r float -> expr r float = sql "SIGN($1)"

## `greatest a b`: the larger of two non-null values, of any ordered type.
## SQLite has no `GREATEST`, so the `CASE` spelling is the portable one; with
## non-null arguments no NULL has to be resolved.
greatest : expr r a -> expr r a -> expr r a = sql "CASE WHEN $1 >= $2 THEN $1 ELSE $2 END"

## `least a b`: the smaller of two non-null values. Portable for the same
## reason as `greatest`.
least : expr r a -> expr r a -> expr r a = sql "CASE WHEN $1 <= $2 THEN $1 ELSE $2 END"

## Render a value as a string, overloading `int`, `float`, `date`, `timestamp`
## and `bool`. The bool form is a `CASE` giving `'true'` / `'false'`, because
## engines disagree about how they print a boolean.
toString : expr r int -> expr r string = sql "CAGARA_TO_STRING($1)"
toString : expr r float -> expr r string = sql "CAGARA_TO_STRING($1)"
toString : expr r date -> expr r string = sql "CAGARA_TO_STRING($1)"
toString : expr r timestamp -> expr r string = sql "CAGARA_TO_STRING($1)"
toString : expr r bool -> expr r string = sql "CASE WHEN $1 THEN 'true' ELSE 'false' END"

# Casts are named for the type they produce. Overloading picks the one that
# fits, so `toInt` applied to a `float` is a narrowing and `toInt` applied to a
# `string` is a parse -- the same destination reached from different sources.

## Convert to `int`: from a `float` it truncates toward zero, in every dialect
## (`CAGARA_TRUNC_INT`); from a `string` it parses an integer and fails on a
## value that is not one (`tryInt` yields NULL instead).
toInt : expr r float -> expr r int = sql "CAGARA_TRUNC_INT($1)"
toInt : expr r string -> expr r int = sql "CAGARA_TO_INT($1)"

## Convert to `float`, a 64-bit IEEE double: from an `int` exactly, from a
## `string` by parsing.
toFloat : expr r int -> expr r float = sql "CAGARA_TO_FLOAT($1)"
toFloat : expr r string -> expr r float = sql "CAGARA_TO_FLOAT($1)"

## Convert to `bool` from a number: zero is false, everything else true.
toBool : expr r int -> expr r bool = sql "CAST($1 AS BOOLEAN)"
toBool : expr r float -> expr r bool = sql "CAST($1 AS BOOLEAN)"

## `toDecimal precision scale x` gives a value an exact SQL type. Money is the
## reason: plain `FLOAT` is binary floating point, so sums of it drift, while
## `DECIMAL(18, 2)` is exact. The *language* type stays `float` — there is no
## separate decimal scalar — so this changes the SQL type without adding one.
## Usable from `string`, `int` and `float` sources.
toDecimal : expr r int -> expr r int -> expr r string -> expr r float =
  sql "CAGARA_TO_DECIMAL($3, $1, $2)"
toDecimal : expr r int -> expr r int -> expr r int -> expr r float =
  sql "CAGARA_TO_DECIMAL($3, $1, $2)"
toDecimal : expr r int -> expr r int -> expr r float -> expr r float =
  sql "CAGARA_TO_DECIMAL($3, $1, $2)"

# A `from*` name says where the value came from rather than where it is going,
# which is the more useful reading at a call site: `fromString .man_grade` says
# the column is text and is being read as a number. Each is the `to*` above,
# so there is one implementation and two ways to name it.

## `fromString` reads a `string` as the type the context asks for — `int`,
## `float` or `date`. The `to*` cast with the direction named from the source.
fromString : expr r string -> expr r int = toInt
fromString : expr r string -> expr r float = toFloat
fromString : expr r string -> expr r date = toDate

## `fromInt` reads an `int` as a `float`: `toFloat` with the direction named
## from the source.
fromInt : expr r int -> expr r float = toFloat

## `fromFloat` reads a `float` as an `int`, truncating toward zero.
fromFloat : expr r float -> expr r int = toInt

# The casts above fail on a value they cannot convert. For a column typed
# `string` that holds a code -- and `icbase` has tens of thousands of those --
# one bad row would otherwise fail the whole query, so the `try*` forms yield
# NULL instead. They return `maybe`, because that NULL has to be handled
# somewhere: `coalesce 0 (tryInt .x)`.

## `tryInt` parses a `string` as an `int`, giving NULL where `toInt` would
## fail. Always `maybe`, so the caller decides what a bad row means.
tryInt : expr r string -> expr r (maybe int) = sql "CAGARA_TRY_INT($1)"

## `tryFloat` parses a `string` as a `float`, NULL on failure.
tryFloat : expr r string -> expr r (maybe float) = sql "CAGARA_TRY_FLOAT($1)"

## `tryDate` parses a `string` as a `date`, NULL on failure.
tryDate : expr r string -> expr r (maybe date) = sql "CAGARA_TRY_DATE($1)"

## `tryTimestamp` parses a `string` as a `timestamp`, NULL on failure.
tryTimestamp : expr r string -> expr r (maybe timestamp) = sql "CAGARA_TRY_TIMESTAMP($1)"

## `tryDecimal precision scale x`: `toDecimal` for a `string` that may not be a
## number, NULL on failure.
tryDecimal : expr r int -> expr r int -> expr r string -> expr r (maybe float) =
  sql "CAGARA_TRY_DECIMAL($3, $1, $2)"

# ── dates and timestamps ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# String literals widen to `date` / `timestamp`: `.created_at >= "2024-01-01"`.
# Weeks start on Monday (ISO). `dayOfWeek` is 0 for Sunday.

## Today, as a `date`.
currentDate : expr r date = sql "CURRENT_DATE"

## The current instant, to the engine's precision.
currentTimestamp : expr r timestamp = sql "CAGARA_NOW()"

## Alias of `currentTimestamp`, under the shorter name.
now : expr r timestamp = currentTimestamp

## The day before today. The most common partition date there is: a table
## partitioned by day is read for the day that has finished, and `yesterday`
## says that in one word at a use site where `addDays (-1) currentDate` is a
## parenthesised expression in the middle of a pipeline.
yesterday : expr r date = addDays (-1) currentDate

## Convert to `date`: from a `timestamp` it truncates to the day, from a
## `string` it parses one.
toDate : expr r timestamp -> expr r date = sql "CAGARA_TO_DATE($1)"
toDate : expr r string -> expr r date = sql "CAGARA_TO_DATE($1)"

## Convert to `timestamp`: from a `date` (midnight) or from a `string`.
toTimestamp : expr r date -> expr r timestamp = sql "CAGARA_TO_TIMESTAMP($1)"
toTimestamp : expr r string -> expr r timestamp = sql "CAGARA_TO_TIMESTAMP($1)"

## `addDays 7 .created_at`: move a date or timestamp by whole days; negative
## counts go back. Overloaded on `date` and `timestamp`, returning the type it
## was given.
##
## The week, quarter and year shifts below are defined from this one: 7 days,
## 3 months, 1 year. So the core needs only `DAY` and `MONTH`.
addDays : expr r int -> expr r date -> expr r date = sql "CAGARA_ADD('DATE', 'DAY', $2, $1)"
addDays : expr r int -> expr r timestamp -> expr r timestamp =
  sql "CAGARA_ADD('TIMESTAMP', 'DAY', $2, $1)"

## `addWeeks 2 .d`: move by whole weeks, spelled as `addDays` of `n * 7`.
addWeeks : expr r int -> expr r date -> expr r date = sql "CAGARA_ADD('DATE', 'DAY', $2, $1 * 7)"
addWeeks : expr r int -> expr r timestamp -> expr r timestamp =
  sql "CAGARA_ADD('TIMESTAMP', 'DAY', $2, $1 * 7)"

## `addMonths 1 .d`: move by calendar months. The engine decides what the 31st
## of a month without a 31st becomes.
addMonths : expr r int -> expr r date -> expr r date = sql "CAGARA_ADD('DATE', 'MONTH', $2, $1)"
addMonths : expr r int -> expr r timestamp -> expr r timestamp =
  sql "CAGARA_ADD('TIMESTAMP', 'MONTH', $2, $1)"

## `addQuarters 1 .d`: move by quarters, spelled as `addMonths` of `n * 3`.
addQuarters : expr r int -> expr r date -> expr r date =
  sql "CAGARA_ADD('DATE', 'MONTH', $2, $1 * 3)"
addQuarters : expr r int -> expr r timestamp -> expr r timestamp =
  sql "CAGARA_ADD('TIMESTAMP', 'MONTH', $2, $1 * 3)"

## `addYears 1 .d`: move by calendar years.
addYears : expr r int -> expr r date -> expr r date = sql "CAGARA_ADD('DATE', 'YEAR', $2, $1)"
addYears : expr r int -> expr r timestamp -> expr r timestamp =
  sql "CAGARA_ADD('TIMESTAMP', 'YEAR', $2, $1)"

## `addHours 2 .t`: move a timestamp by whole hours. Timestamps only — a `date`
## has no hour to move.
addHours : expr r int -> expr r timestamp -> expr r timestamp =
  sql "CAGARA_ADD('TIMESTAMP', 'HOUR', $2, $1)"

## `addMinutes 30 .t`: move a timestamp by whole minutes.
addMinutes : expr r int -> expr r timestamp -> expr r timestamp =
  sql "CAGARA_ADD('TIMESTAMP', 'MINUTE', $2, $1)"

## `addSeconds 30 .t`: move a timestamp by whole seconds.
addSeconds : expr r int -> expr r timestamp -> expr r timestamp =
  sql "CAGARA_ADD('TIMESTAMP', 'SECOND', $2, $1)"

# date_trunc: the start of the period containing the value, same type.

## `truncYear .d`: the first instant of the containing year, in the input's own
## type.
truncYear : expr r date -> expr r date = sql "CAGARA_TRUNC('DATE', 'YEAR', $1)"
truncYear : expr r timestamp -> expr r timestamp = sql "CAGARA_TRUNC('TIMESTAMP', 'YEAR', $1)"

## `truncQuarter .d`: the first day of the containing quarter.
truncQuarter : expr r date -> expr r date = sql "CAGARA_TRUNC('DATE', 'QUARTER', $1)"
truncQuarter : expr r timestamp -> expr r timestamp = sql "CAGARA_TRUNC('TIMESTAMP', 'QUARTER', $1)"

## `truncMonth .d`: the first day of the containing month — the usual spelling
## of a monthly bucket.
truncMonth : expr r date -> expr r date = sql "CAGARA_TRUNC('DATE', 'MONTH', $1)"
truncMonth : expr r timestamp -> expr r timestamp = sql "CAGARA_TRUNC('TIMESTAMP', 'MONTH', $1)"

## `truncWeek .d`: the Monday of the containing week.
truncWeek : expr r date -> expr r date = sql "CAGARA_TRUNC('DATE', 'WEEK', $1)"
truncWeek : expr r timestamp -> expr r timestamp = sql "CAGARA_TRUNC('TIMESTAMP', 'WEEK', $1)"

## `truncDay .t`: midnight of the containing day, as a timestamp.
truncDay : expr r timestamp -> expr r timestamp = sql "CAGARA_TRUNC('TIMESTAMP', 'DAY', $1)"

## `truncHour .t`: the start of the containing hour.
truncHour : expr r timestamp -> expr r timestamp = sql "CAGARA_TRUNC('TIMESTAMP', 'HOUR', $1)"

## `truncMinute .t`: the start of the containing minute.
truncMinute : expr r timestamp -> expr r timestamp = sql "CAGARA_TRUNC('TIMESTAMP', 'MINUTE', $1)"

# Fields as integers.

## The year of a date or timestamp: `year .created_at`.
year : expr r date -> expr r int = sql "CAGARA_PART('DATE', 'YEAR', $1)"
year : expr r timestamp -> expr r int = sql "CAGARA_PART('TIMESTAMP', 'YEAR', $1)"

## The quarter, 1–4.
quarter : expr r date -> expr r int = sql "CAGARA_PART('DATE', 'QUARTER', $1)"
quarter : expr r timestamp -> expr r int = sql "CAGARA_PART('TIMESTAMP', 'QUARTER', $1)"

## The month, 1–12.
month : expr r date -> expr r int = sql "CAGARA_PART('DATE', 'MONTH', $1)"
month : expr r timestamp -> expr r int = sql "CAGARA_PART('TIMESTAMP', 'MONTH', $1)"

## The day of the month, 1–31.
day : expr r date -> expr r int = sql "CAGARA_PART('DATE', 'DAY', $1)"
day : expr r timestamp -> expr r int = sql "CAGARA_PART('TIMESTAMP', 'DAY', $1)"

## The day of the week, 0 for Sunday — the numbering SQL engines use, not
## ISO's.
dayOfWeek : expr r date -> expr r int = sql "CAGARA_PART('DATE', 'DOW', $1)"
dayOfWeek : expr r timestamp -> expr r int = sql "CAGARA_PART('TIMESTAMP', 'DOW', $1)"

## The day of the year, 1–366.
dayOfYear : expr r date -> expr r int = sql "CAGARA_PART('DATE', 'DOY', $1)"
dayOfYear : expr r timestamp -> expr r int = sql "CAGARA_PART('TIMESTAMP', 'DOY', $1)"

## The hour, 0–23, of a timestamp.
hour : expr r timestamp -> expr r int = sql "CAGARA_PART('TIMESTAMP', 'HOUR', $1)"

## The minute, 0–59, of a timestamp.
minute : expr r timestamp -> expr r int = sql "CAGARA_PART('TIMESTAMP', 'MINUTE', $1)"

## `daysBetween start end`: whole days from `start` to `end`, negative if `end`
## is earlier. Subject last, so `daysBetween .created_at` is a function of the
## end — an age in days.
daysBetween : expr r date -> expr r date -> expr r int = sql "CAGARA_DAYS_BETWEEN($1, $2)"

# ── nulls: operators need non-null arguments, so nulls are handled here ────

## `coalesce default x`, like Haskell's `fromMaybe`: `x` if it is not NULL, the
## default otherwise. Default first and subject last, so `coalesce 0` is a
## reusable "or zero".
##
## The infix form is `x ?? default` (right-associative, tighter than arithmetic:
## `.a ?? .b ?? 0`, `.n ?? 0 + 1`).
coalesce : expr r a -> expr r (maybe a) -> expr r a = sql "COALESCE($2, $1)"

## `x ?? default` is `coalesce default x`, written with the nullable value
## first. Right-associative, so `.a ?? .b ?? 0` chains.
_??_ : expr r (maybe a) -> expr r a -> expr r a = sql "COALESCE($1, $2)"

## Lift a non-null value into `maybe`. It does not create NULL — that is
## `nothing` — so `just x` is the "this one is present" spelling in a record of
## optional fields.
just : expr r a -> expr r (maybe a) = sql "$1"

## A SQL NULL at any nullable type, in any phase. The only way to write NULL:
## every other expression is non-null by its signature.
nothing : expr r (maybe a) = sql "NULL"

## `nullIf a b`: NULL when `a` equals `b`, `a` otherwise — how a sentinel value
## is turned into a real NULL.
nullIf : expr r a -> expr r a -> expr r (maybe a) = sql "NULLIF($1, $2)"

## Whether a nullable value is NULL. The result is non-null `bool`, which is
## what makes it usable in `where`.
isNull : expr r (maybe a) -> expr r bool = sql "$1 IS NULL"

## Whether a nullable value is not NULL; non-null `bool`.
isNotNull : expr r (maybe a) -> expr r bool = sql "$1 IS NOT NULL"

## A nullable `bool` as a plain one, with NULL read as false — the same choice
## `where` makes, stated where the value is used rather than where it is
## filtered.
isTrue : expr r (maybe bool) -> expr r bool = sql "COALESCE($1, FALSE)"

## `whereTrue pred q`: `where` for a nullable predicate, with NULL treated as
## false. A nullable predicate has SQL's three-valued result, so this makes the
## usual filtering choice explicit instead of surprising.
whereTrue : expr r (maybe bool) -> query r -> query r = pred => q => where (isTrue pred) q

## Equality that treats two NULL values as equal, and never returns NULL — so
## it can be used directly in `where` and in join predicates, where a plain
## `==` between two nullables would filter everything out.
eqMaybe : expr r (maybe a) -> expr r (maybe a) -> expr r bool =
  sql "COALESCE(($1 = $2) OR ($1 IS NULL AND $2 IS NULL), FALSE)"
